<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel ibn Naghrela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">993–1056</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Samuel ha-Nagid, Shmuel HaNagid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel ibn Naghrela (993–1056), known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as Samuel ha-Nagid, was a Jewish scholar, poet and statesman of the Zirid kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to King Badis and led Granada's armies in the field. He was one of the great poets of medieval Hebrew literature, a Talmudic authority and the leader of the Jewish community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p></div>
